mips: read narrow closure returns from the low-order end of the slot

A closure hands its return value back through an FFI_SIZEOF_ARG-byte slot at
V0_OFF2, and the n32/n64 epilogue loads the value out of that slot with a load
sized to the return type.  Every one of those narrow loads reads from offset 0.
On a little-endian target that is where the value is; on a big-endian one it is
the *high* half of the word, so a closure returning int hands back the top 32
bits of its own result:

    f(42)   -> 0      0x000000000000002A, high half = 0
    f(1000) -> 0      0x00000000000003E8, high half = 0
    f(-7)   -> -1     0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF9, high half = -1

Only closures are affected -- ffi_call's own narrow returns store a full,
already-extended ffi_arg at offset 0 (see retsint32 and friends above), and
64-bit closure returns use a full-width ld.  mips64el is unaffected either way,
which is why this has stood: the fault needs n32/n64 *and* big-endian.

The low-order end is the offset the rest of the world agrees on.  It is where a
caller following libffi's documented `*(ffi_arg *)ret = value` convention leaves
the significant bytes, it is where CPython's ctypes writes a narrow callback
result (Modules/_ctypes/callbacks.c, under WORDS_BIGENDIAN), and it is what the
other big-endian ports -- s390x, powerpc64 -- already read.  Those two writers
land on the same bytes on a big-endian target, so one offset serves both.

Found via test.test_ctypes.test_simplesubclasses on mips64-linux-musl, where
every c_int callback returned 0.

--- a/src/mips/n32.S
+++ b/src/mips/n32.S
@@ -524,6 +524,15 @@
 #define RA_OFF2		(1  * FFI_SIZEOF_ARG)
 #define GP_OFF2		(0  * FFI_SIZEOF_ARG)
 
+/* A narrow return sits at the low-order end of the FFI_SIZEOF_ARG-byte slot the
+   closure wrote: offset 0 little-endian, the far end big-endian.  Loading from
+   offset 0 unconditionally hands back the high half on a big-endian target.  */
+#if defined(__MIPSEB__) || defined(_MIPSEB)
+# define V0_OFF2_N(size)	(V0_OFF2 + FFI_SIZEOF_ARG - (size))
+#else
+# define V0_OFF2_N(size)	V0_OFF2
+#endif
+
 	.align	2
 	.globl	ffi_go_closure_N32
 	.ent	ffi_go_closure_N32
@@ -628,27 +642,27 @@
 
 cls_retsint32:
 	bne     v0, FFI_TYPE_SINT32, cls_retsint16
-	lw	v0, V0_OFF2($sp)
+	lw	v0, V0_OFF2_N(4)($sp)
 	b	cls_epilogue
 
 cls_retsint16:
 	bne     v0, FFI_TYPE_SINT16, cls_retuint16
-	lh	v0, V0_OFF2($sp)
+	lh	v0, V0_OFF2_N(2)($sp)
 	b	cls_epilogue
 
 cls_retuint16:
 	bne     v0, FFI_TYPE_UINT16, cls_retsint8
-	lhu	v0, V0_OFF2($sp)
+	lhu	v0, V0_OFF2_N(2)($sp)
 	b	cls_epilogue
 
 cls_retsint8:
 	bne     v0, FFI_TYPE_SINT8, cls_retuint8
-	lb	v0, V0_OFF2($sp)
+	lb	v0, V0_OFF2_N(1)($sp)
 	b	cls_epilogue
 
 cls_retuint8:
 	bne     v0, FFI_TYPE_UINT8, cls_retfloat
-	lbu	v0, V0_OFF2($sp)
+	lbu	v0, V0_OFF2_N(1)($sp)
 	b	cls_epilogue
 
 cls_retfloat:
